The U.S. state of [[Ohio]] first required its residents to register their [[motor vehicle]]s and display [[Vehicle registration plate|license plate]]s in 1908, although several cities within the state issued their own license plates from as early as 1902.

{{As of|2025}}, plates are issued by the Ohio Bureau of Motor Vehicles (BMV), a division of the [[Ohio Department of Public Safety]]. Only rear plates have been required for all classes of vehicles, except commercial tractors, since July 1, 2020.<ref name="Bischoff">{{cite news |last=Bischoff |first=Laura A. |date=April 3, 2019 |title=No more front license plate: Gov. DeWine signs bill doing away with it |url=https://www.daytondailynews.com/news/local/more-front-license-plate-ohio-lawmakers-reach-deal-drop-requirement/2IEbegbFrI7XwKgpL4CS8L/ |newspaper=[[Dayton Daily News]] |access-date=July 9, 2019}}</ref> However, vehicle owners can request specialized, personalized plate serials, in addition to their rear plates, for an extra charge. But eventually, that second-plate option will phase out entirely when state legislation passes a bill. <ref>{{cite web |last=Braddock |first=Tiarra |date=July 1, 2020 |title=Ohio drivers no longer required to have front license plates |url=https://www.cleveland19.com/2020/07/01/ohio-drivers-no-longer-required-have-front-license-plates/ |publisher=[[WOIO]] |access-date=November 1, 2021 |quote=Drivers can still request a second license plate for an extra $7.50, but a single plate will cost $6.50.}}</ref> All plates are manufactured by inmates at Ohio Penal Industries at the [[Lebanon Correctional Institution]];<ref name="Flat">{{cite press release |url=http://www.state.oh.us/odps/division/bmv/0803News.html#Flat_Plate |title="Flat" License Plates Now Issued For Made-To-Order Plates |publisher=Bureau of Motor Vehicles, [[Ohio Department of Public Safety]] |date=August 12, 2003 |access-date=December 2, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20031202152828/http://www.state.oh.us/odps/division/bmv/0803News.html#Flat_Plate |archive-date=December 2, 2003 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name="Enquirer Pulfer" /> since 2015, they have been manufactured out of [[aluminum]], having been made of galvanized steel beforehand.<ref name="Blackwell">{{cite news|last=Blackwell |first=Brandon |date=January 29, 2013 |title=Ohio license plate recall brings more questions than answers |url=https://www.cleveland.com/metro/index.ssf/2013/01/ohio_license_plate_recall_brin.html |newspaper=[[The Plain Dealer]] |publisher=[[Advance Publications]] |access-date=September 8, 2013}}</ref> The BMV issues a new license plate design about every five years,<ref name="Blade">{{cite news|title=Beautiful Ohio ousts sunburst as state plate |url=https://www.toledoblade.com/State/2010/06/07/Beautiful-Ohio-ousts-sunburst-as-state-plate.html |newspaper=[[The Blade (Toledo, Ohio)|The Blade]] |location=[[Toledo, Ohio]] |publisher=[[Block Communications]] |date=June 7, 2010 |access-date=September 5, 2013}}</ref> or with each new administration in the state government.<ref name="McNair" />

A new "Sunrise in Ohio" plate design was unveiled by Governor [[Mike DeWine]] on October 21, 2021,<ref>{{cite web |last=Tobias |first=Andrew J. |date=October 21, 2021 |title=Ohio unveils new 'Sunrise in Ohio' license plate design |url=https://www.cleveland.com/open/2021/10/ohio-unveils-new-sunrise-in-ohio-license-plate-design.html |website=Cleveland.com |publisher=[[Advance Publications]] |access-date=December 30, 2021}}</ref> and was made available to drivers December 29, 2021, replacing the "Ohio Pride" design which had been issued since April 2013.<ref name="WTVG">{{cite web |last=Croup |first=Josh |date=December 26, 2021 |title=New Ohio license plate design becomes standard Wednesday |url=https://www.13abc.com/2021/12/26/new-ohio-license-plate-design-becomes-standard-wednesday/ |publisher=[[WTVG]] |access-date=December 30, 2021}}</ref>

==History==
On May 19, 1902, [[Cleveland]] became one of the first cities in the country to require motorists to display government-issued registration numbers on their vehicles.<ref name="Tanner">{{cite web |last=Tanner |first=Eric N. |title=Ohio Pre-state Local License Plates |url=https://allaboutlicenseplates.com/displayPage.asp?country=US&p=PrestateLocal&jurisdiction=Ohio&code=oh |website=allaboutlicenseplates.com |access-date=September 13, 2021}}</ref><ref name="Raiche">{{cite web |last=Raiche |first=Steve |title=Old Ohio License Plates |url=https://leatherlicenseplates.com/old-ohio-license-plates-vintage-ohio-license-plates/ |website=LeatherLicensePlates.com |date=August 28, 2015 |access-date=May 21, 2017}}</ref> In the following years, various local governments in Ohio issued standard metal plates of varying design or numerals (to be mounted on a dark background), including:

{{div col}}
*[[Canton, Ohio|Canton]] (1905)<ref name="Tanner" />
*[[Cleveland]] (1907–08)<ref name="Tanner" />
*[[Cincinnati]] (initials only 1903–05; 1906–08; motorcycles in 1911, 1913<ref name="Tanner" />), abbreviated "Cinti"<ref name="Raiche" />
*[[Columbus, Ohio|Columbus]] (1907–08), abbreviated "Col's"<ref name="Raiche" />
*[[Dayton, Ohio|Dayton]] (1905, 1907, 1908;<ref name="Raiche" /> motorcycles in 1909, 1912, 1913<ref name="Taylor" />)
*[[Delhi Township, Ohio|Delhi Township, Hamilton County]] (1906–08), abbreviated "Delhi"<ref name="Raiche" />
*[[East Liverpool, Ohio|East Liverpool]] (Health Department vehicles in 1924)<ref name="Taylor" />
*[[Elyria, Ohio|Elyria]] (motorcycles in 1910)<ref name="Tanner" />
*[[Hamilton, Ohio|Hamilton]] (1907), abbreviated "Haml"<ref name="Tanner" />
*[[Lima, Ohio|Lima]] (motorcycles in 1908, 1912–13)<ref name="Tanner" />
*[[Lorain, Ohio|Lorain]] (1907)<ref name="Taylor" />
*[[Mansfield, Ohio|Mansfield]] (1903)<ref name="Tanner" />
*[[Massillon, Ohio|Massillon]] (1906<ref name="Tanner" />), abbreviated "M"<ref name="Raiche" />
*[[Newark, Ohio|Newark]] (motorcycles in 1912–13)<ref name="Tanner" />
*[[Sandusky, Ohio|Sandusky]] (1903)<ref name="Tanner" />
*[[Springfield, Ohio|Springfield]] (unknown; motorcycles in 1913), abbreviated "Spfd" on motorcycles<ref name="Tanner" />
*[[Toledo, Ohio|Toledo]] (1904, 1907)<ref name="Tanner" />
*[[Warren, Ohio|Warren]] (1908)<ref name="Raiche" />
{{div col end}}

In 1906, the state attempted to take over auto registration under the Ward Automobile Law, but litigation delayed the program until the [[Supreme Court of Ohio|Ohio Supreme Court]] ruled in favor of the law. The [[Ohio Secretary of State]]'s Automobile Division, precursor to the Bureau of Motor Vehicles, was established in 1907.<ref name="BMV history outline">{{cite web|title=ODPS Ohio Bureau of Motor Vehicles History|publisher=[[Ohio Bureau of Motor Vehicles]]|access-date=September 8, 2013|url=http://www.bmv.ohio.gov/history.stm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140714194533/http://www.bmv.ohio.gov/history.stm|archive-date=July 14, 2014|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref> The Ward Law went into effect on June 11, 1908, but the Automobile Division did not begin issuing plates for another 30 days due to a manufacturing defect.<ref name="Taylor" /> The first state vehicle registration was issued to Cincinnati resident Thomas B. Paxton, Jr., for his [[Franklin (automobile)|Franklin]] automobile.<ref name="BMV history" /> Locally issued and owner-provided license plates were phased out by 1909 for automobiles,<ref name="Raiche" /> but local plates continued to be used for motorcycles until 1914.<ref name="Taylor">{{cite web|url=https://porcelainplates.net/ohio_archive.html |title=Ohio Archive |last=Taylor |first=Eric Robert |website=PorcelainPlates.net |date=January 20, 2013 |access-date=September 9, 2013}}</ref> One effect of the Ward Law was to eliminate a significant revenue stream for cities like Cincinnati, which took in about $5,000 a year (equivalent to ${{formatnum:{{inflation|US|5000|1909|r=-3}}}} today) from auto registrations.<ref name="Horseless Age">{{cite journal|title=Minor Mention|journal=The Horseless Age|volume=21|publisher=Horseless Age Company|date=May 20, 1908|page=617|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=2GY6AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA617|quote=Under the new Ward automobile law, which has recently passed into effect in Ohio, the cities cannot license automobiles, and lose thereby a substantial income, Cincinnati, for instance, about $5,000 a year.}}</ref>

The Ward Law required automobile owners to display plates at both the front and the rear of the vehicle. Front and rear plates would be issued for passenger vehicles for over a century, through June 30, 2020, with the exception of 1944–46 when only rear plates were issued due to metal conservation for [[World War II]].<ref name="Bischoff"/> Ohio issued single-year plates from 1910 through 1973, except in 1943 and 1952 when windshield stickers were issued to revalidate the previous year's plates, again due to metal conservation (for World War II and the [[Korean War]] respectively).

Various Ohio license plate designs from 1908 to 1921 used distinctive monograms instead of a fully spelled-out state name.<ref name="BMV history">{{cite book |title=Ohio BMV Chronological History 1908-2010 |publisher=Ohio Bureau of Motor Vehicles |year=2011 |access-date=September 9, 2013 |url=http://www.publicsafety.ohio.gov/links/bmv_history.pdf }}</ref> The 1938 plate commemorated the 150th anniversary of the creation of the [[Northwest Territory]] (from which the state of Ohio was formed), and thus was the first plate in the state to feature a graphic and a slogan. In 1953, the Bureau of Motor Vehicles commemorated the state's sesquicentennial by issuing a special front plate bearing the state shape and the word "sesqui-centennial" {{sic}} instead of the passenger serial, which was carried only on the rear plate.

[[File:Golf cart with license plate.jpg|thumb|right|upright|A [[golf cart]] in [[Put-in-Bay, Ohio|Put-in-Bay]] displaying an Ohio Bicentennial passenger plate.]]

From 1935 through 1979, serials were allocated in blocks to each of the state's 88 counties. Serials were originally up to five characters in length and featured one or two letters. Increased demand resulted in the introduction of six-character serials in 1949, followed by all-numeric serials in 1962. The scheme was finally abandoned in 1980.<ref name="allaboutlicenseplatesOH">{{cite web |last=Tanner |first=Eric N. |title=Ohio Passenger License Plates |url=https://allaboutlicenseplates.com/displayPage.asp?country=US&p=Passenger&jurisdiction=Ohio&code=oh |publisher=allaboutlicenseplates.com |access-date=November 17, 2020}}</ref>

In 1956, the United States, Canada, and Mexico came to an agreement with the [[American Association of Motor Vehicle Administrators]], the [[Automobile Manufacturers Association]] and the [[National Safety Council]] that standardized the size for license plates for vehicles (except those for [[motorcycle]]s) at {{convert|6|in|cm}} in height by {{convert|12|in|cm}} in width, with standardized mounting holes.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Garrish |first=Christopher |date=October 2016 |title=Reconsidering the Standard Plate Size |magazine=Plates |publisher=[[Automobile License Plate Collectors Association]] |volume=62 |number=5}}</ref> The 1956 (dated 1957) issue was the first Ohio license plate that fully complied with these standards: the state had been issuing plates 6 inches in height by 12 inches in width since 1926, and all plates of the 1952 (dated 1953) and 1955 (dated 1956) issues were to these dimensions, but none had had standard mounting holes.<ref>{{cite web |last=Tanner |first=Eric N. |title=Ohio License Plate Sizes |url=https://allaboutlicenseplates.com/displayPage.asp?country=US&p=Sizes&jurisdiction=Ohio&code=oh |publisher=allaboutlicenseplates.com |access-date=November 17, 2020}}</ref>

In 1967, the state began issuing special plates to [[Driving under the influence|DUI]] offenders with limited driving privileges. Judges in Ohio, however, rarely issued them until a 2004 state law made it mandatory for all DUI offenders with limited driving privileges to have them.<ref name="ABC DUI">{{cite web |last=Shaw |first=Stephen |date=February 13, 2015 |title=Ohio's DUI Scarlet Letter Plates |url=https://duischoolnv.com/ohios-dui-scarlet-letter-plates/ |website=ABC DUI Online |publisher=Shaw Education Development |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150517002452/http://duischoolnv.com/ohios-dui-scarlet-letter-license-plates/ |archive-date=May 17, 2015 |access-date=May 13, 2015}}</ref>

After the last single-year plate was issued in 1973, the 1974 plate was revalidated for 1975 with a sticker placed at the bottom right corner. The first undated, multi-year plate was issued in 1976, while monthly staggered registration was introduced in 1979.<ref name="BMV history" />

Since 1983, plates have carried the county of issuance on a sticker. Originally, this was a long sticker centered at the bottom of the plate, displaying the county name.<ref name="Nicholson">{{cite web |last=Nicholson |first=David |title=Ohio License Plates, 1969–present |url=https://www.15q.net/oh.html |website=15q.net |date=June 15, 2013 |access-date=September 8, 2013 |archive-date=September 10, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130910133120/http://www.15q.net/oh.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> In 1992, the state introduced a numerical county-coding scheme (see the County Coding section below), with the county number being displayed on a red sticker at the bottom left corner of the plate; this scheme was initially used only on specialty plates before being adopted on standard passenger plates when the Bicentennial base was introduced in October 2001.<ref name="Enquirer Pulfer">{{cite news |last=Pulfer |first=Mike |date=June 3, 2002 |title=Ask A Stupid Question |url=http://www.enquirer.com/editions/2002/06/03/tem_ask_a_stupid.html |newspaper=[[The Cincinnati Enquirer]] |publisher=[[Gannett Company]] |access-date=September 8, 2013}}</ref> The scheme was discontinued on standard passenger plates in 2018 in favor of a return to county-name stickers, again centered at the bottom of the plate.<ref name="Nicholson"/> The county number sticker can still be requested for standard passenger plates at a BMV office in place of a county-name sticker.

On April 3, 2019, Governor [[Mike DeWine]] signed a two-year state transportation budget bill that included the elimination of the requirement for front license plates to be displayed. This became effective on July 1, 2020.<ref name="Bischoff"/>

